The extension establishes finite-test expansion, exact pushforward, and normalized Wilson integration, including finite link products. The link specialization uses the supplied probability instance and requires no Haar invariance. Reflection positivity enters the quotient result as a hypothesis on the pulled-back test. Wilson cross-plane factorization, independent quotient densities, Markov dynamics, continuum reconstruction, Hamiltonian identification, and a mass gap remain outside the scope.

